{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. It MAY name another bundled skill to use instead where an agent could confuse the two. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query `requirements.cli.skill_conventions/skills`.**\n- Running it selects exactly five files: the `SKILL.md` files for `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-refactor` and `habeas-bootstrap-requirements`.\n- Each of the five has front matter with a `name:` line. A search for `^name: ` across the project found only these five, so the query neither misses a skill nor selects anything else.\n- `assets/skills` has only these five directories, and the only `---` fences in it are these files' front-matter delimiters.\n- The query's `match` snippet is front matter followed by a heading. Its `no_match` snippet is a heading with no front matter, so the snippets show both what the query selects and the nearby text it must not select.\n\n**Obligations.**\n- The MUST is that each skill's description states when to use it, in terms of the user's task.\n- The MAY, naming another skill to use instead, imposes nothing to check.\n- Each skill is a site, and each site's own review covers it.\n- The requirement is also checked by the test below, which I assess here.\n\n**Test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_descriptions_say_when_to_use_them]`.**\n- It loops over `SKILLS`, which another test (`bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics`) compares with the directories on disk. It asserts that the front matter has exactly one `name` equal to the directory name, and exactly one non-empty `description` of at most 1024 characters, matching the helper `description(name)`.\n- For all five skills it asserts that the description starts with a \"Use …\" phrase tied to the user's task. Examples are \"Use when adopting Habeas in an existing codebase\" and \"Use when a change moves code, tests, or requirements\". A description that omitted the when-to-use statement, or put it elsewhere, would fail.\n- It checks that any `habeas-*` skill a description names is a bundled one. This guards the optional \"use instead\" pointer.\n- It asserts that the confusable pairs name the right alternative: requirements→refactor and evidence, evidence→refactor, bootstrap→evidence, refactor→requirements. This covers the MAY clause.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. The when-to-use obligation is tested for every site with a positive assertion. The optional clause is also tested. The query is heuristic and well scoped. No acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "4fbf9a3072386612109271b7cf55673834816c2318c1c11463d91326147378e5",
